1781	avalon_20251201_044722	proposal	100403	Diana	good	\N	Mission 1 (Charlie, Diana) succeeded with 0 fails, which strongly suggests both Charlie and I are good, since two evils voluntarily passing on the first mission is unlikely. Mission 2 (Diana, Alice, Eve) had exactly 1 fail, so the single evil there must be in {Alice, Eve} because I know I’m good. Mission 3 (Bob, Charlie) also had exactly 1 fail, so there is exactly one evil in {Bob, Charlie}. With only 2 total evils in the game, that means the evils are one from {Alice, Eve} and one from {Bob, Charlie}. Eve’s discussion logic was suspiciously self-exonerating (asserting ‘exactly one evil in {Alice, Diana}’ without justification and excluding herself), which makes her more likely to be the evil in {Alice, Eve}, leaving Alice good. Combining that with Charlie’s strong prior from the clean Mission 1 makes Bob the more likely evil in {Bob, Charlie}. Therefore the most consistent all-good team is [Diana, Charlie, Alice], which avoids both likely evils Bob and Eve and does not repeat either of the failing pairs (Diana–Eve from M2 or Bob–Charlie from M3).	[-0.014529882,-0.020480957,0.031754788,-0.041832432,-0.0013669597,-0.020669848,-0.023074003,-0.06557239,0.0005059964,0.039316375,-0.04161646,-0.021945754,0.065761484,-0.08642873,0.017303813,0.0436253,0.019162703,0.032413844,-0.044192914,0.028971704,-0.06489854,-0.04284521,-0.003741558,0.00936102,-0.031343013,0.0802071,-0.022805,-0.013800399,0.0021590008,-0.12470196,-0.01047808,0.05728188,0.025214372,0.022445517,0.019961322,0.048567493,-0.024789141,-0.0269551,0.0037021195,0.025993237,-0.02354873,-0.010674764,-0.037020866,-0.053315803,-0.0801476,0.0065704663,0.0026563324,0.027856015,0.06978938,-0.106258675,-0.0135943405,0.019110601,-0.03532895,0.024638964,0.022838447,-0.009061858,-0.04416273,0.025409782,-0.03185097,-0.003251746,-0.009192264,0.054382753,-0.02934789,0.0081633115,0.109932505,0.018507328,-0.03468941,-0.00091505965,-0.06516113,0.058035254,0.003936685,-0.0