1886	avalon_20251201_050750	proposal	100400	Eve	good	\N	From the mission results, Mission 2 (Alice, Diana, Eve) having exactly 1 FAIL and Mission 3 (Bob, Charlie) having exactly 1 FAIL implies there is exactly one evil in {Alice, Diana} and exactly one evil in {Bob, Charlie}. I know I am good, so the structure is: 1 evil among {Alice, Diana}, 1 evil among {Bob, Charlie}, and Eve good. That means any team including me and one from each suspect pair risks at most 2 evils and in some worlds only 1. Between the two contested options—Alice/Bob/Eve vs Bob/Diana/Eve—both are structurally similar in risk, but the discussion makes Diana look worse: both Bob and Diana were pushing hard for Bob/Diana/Eve, which is exactly what aligned evils would want if they are paired. Alice, on the other hand, has consistently advocated avoiding Charlie and Diana and specifically suggested Alice/Bob/Eve before I did, which is more consistent with a good perspective given the overlaps. Including Charlie is both socially unlikely to pass and doesn’t reduce theoretical risk given we know exactly one of {Bob, Charlie} is evil. Therefore, Alice/Bob/Eve is the best-balanced, most likely-to-pass team that avoids the more suspicious Diana while still fitting the mission logic.	[-0.032704674,-0.00888087,-0.042672053,-0.062379193,0.016477097,0.03167458,-0.029564222,-0.0117701385,0.034078486,0.055462517,-0.048524093,-0.040885214,0.094155274,-0.05754706,0.025994755,0.03839189,-0.006179991,0.04824728,-0.03177726,0.093511924,-0.06714419,-0.06938516,-0.021125924,-0.0009793781,-0.055352878,0.079118416,0.012824529,0.013343188,-0.030308262,-0.10931363,0.0012651491,0.044182647,0.033875123,0.031869724,-0.006572017,0.006774196,-0.020454135,-0.017717784,0.015528874,-0.0027782472,-0.025907708,-0.0027253146,-0.012695659,-0.0730442,-0.09716965,0.0058747693,0.0009088041,-0.005981383,0.014682134,-0.08771244,-0.01915054,-0.0004763236,-0.03380009,-0.00026113063,-0.011424172,0.02154904,-0.06527507,0.008987391,-0.0276515